    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d2f64be_f15ab952",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 34
      },
      "lineNbr": 7632,
      "author": {
        "id": 1463964
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-03T23:23:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done.\n\nChatted with Ben offline and clarified the javadocs for NetworkCapabilities#setAdministratorUids to emphasize that the caller needs to include the owner UID when setting administrator UIDs.\n\nAdditionally, I think we should consider what the correct behavior is for setting the owner UID - do we need to clear the previous UID from administratorUids? In this case, is it possible for an app to stop being the owner but retain administrator privileges?",
      "parentUuid": "9b6e34d7_c12cb438",
      "revId": "d87c62ca3f6c497ac288dd6ca6d5ca5ab3cf6dd8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f2e1c85_24aeaf9d",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 34
      },
      "lineNbr": 7632,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-04T11:57:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the only party that can meaningfully set these is the party that knows who owns and who administrates this network (in this case, telephony). Wouldn\u0027t it be possible that two UIDs administrate a network, one of them owns it, and transfers ownership to the other and should retain administrative privilege ? I think this is possible in principle, so I don\u0027t think NC should remove the UID from the list of administrator just because it lost owner.\n\nYou could, optionally, decide on a policy for enforcing the presence of the owner UID in the administrators (either add it forcefully when owner is set, or forcefully introduce the owner UID when the administrator is sent, but not both) and then document it carefully. But I think the most sensical thing to do is to say that making sure it\u0027s correctly set is the responsibility of the party who sets them, and that setting them incorrectly leads to undefined behavior. At least that\u0027s easy to understand and the responsibility is clear.\n\nWhat that would mean for this change is : you don\u0027t need to change anything :)",
      "parentUuid": "2d2f64be_f15ab952",
      "revId": "d87c62ca3f6c497ac288dd6ca6d5ca5ab3cf6dd8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c97cd456_1395713e",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
